
	  distcc -- a free distributed C/C++ compiler system

		   https://github.com/distcc/distcc

		    by Martin Pool

		    "pump" functionality added by
 Fergus Henderson, Nils Klarlund, Manos Renieris, and Craig Silverstein
			    (Google Inc.)

distcc is a program to distribute compilation of C or C++ code across
several machines on a network. distcc should always generate the same
results as a local compile, is simple to install and use, and is often
two or more times faster than a local compile.

Unlike other distributed build systems, distcc does not require all
machines to share a filesystem, have synchronized clocks, or to have
the same libraries or header files installed. Machines can be running
different operating systems, as long as they have compatible binary
formats or cross-compilers.

By default, distcc sends the complete preprocessed source code across
the network for each job, so all it requires of the volunteer machines
is that they be running the distccd daemon, and that they have an
appropriate compiler installed.

On Linux, a local `distccd --broker /absolute/socket/path` can share adaptive
host selection and statistics across clients. Set DISTCC_BROKER to that
socket path in the build environment. The broker passes connected sockets to
clients for the default TCP transport. Completed TCP
sessions are pooled when the server supports reuse; idle connections do not
reserve compiler workers. It exits when
idle; systemd socket activation can restart it on demand, including for
clients in a network sandbox. See distccd(1) for configuration and limits.
Example systemd units are in packaging/systemd. For a Portage client, enable
distcc-broker@portage.socket and set
DISTCC_BROKER=/run/distcc-broker-portage.socket in its build environment.

The experimental `,h2` host option multiplexes jobs over one HTTP/2 TCP connection
per worker host. It requires the broker, upgraded workers and libnghttp2.
The broker and daemon own HTTP messages and pass immutable file descriptors
to clients and workers. File bodies use sendfile; no per-job socketpair relays
the payload. Receive storage and flow control are bounded. Closing a client
lease cancels its job; a broken connection can fail all jobs using it.
This is cleartext HTTP/2 on the existing distccd port, subject to its IP ACL;
SSH and GSSAPI are not supported with this option. It is opt-in because fewer
connections do not necessarily mean faster builds or lower client CPU use.
The native resource exchange reduces relay CPU for large inputs, but its
additional HTTP requests can increase latency for small jobs.

`,h2` sends pump's immutable LZ4 snapshots directly. Clients compress
preprocessed inputs and link archives; workers compress results. LZ4 bodies
use sealed memfds and `Content-Encoding: x-distcc-lz4-stream`; compression
is skipped when it does not reduce the size. The broker relays these FDs
without compression or decompression. `,lz4s` uses chunked LZ4 over
pooled TCP. Both options support pump and distributed links. HTTP/2 is not
wire compatible with the previous RPC tunnel: update clients, brokers and
workers together and restart pump sessions. Existing `,lz4` and `,lzo` hosts
retain their TCP protocols.

With `,h2` and pump, an XXH3-64 input manifest identifies cacheable snapshots;
only missing files are uploaded. Small inputs and disabled caches transfer all
required files through the same HTTP resources. Cache hits are verified and copied or
reflinked into private job files; a compiler never shares writable cache
inodes. See doc/protocol-6.txt for the exchange, storage limits and
DISTCCD_FILE_CACHE setting. XXH3-64 detects accidental changes but is not
collision-resistant against adversarial inputs. Build dependencies include
xxHash; pump needs Python xxhash. Update clients and servers together, restart
brokers and pump sessions, and clear old input caches when upgrading this protocol.

GCC and Clang/LLD object links can also run on a broker-selected worker. `distcc-link --sync`
populates immutable sysroot caches using compressed rsync transfers; set
DISTCC_LINK_SYSROOT to the resulting client profile and DISTCCD_LINK_ROOT on
workers to enable this optional path. A profile directory containing gcc.json
and clang.json selects the compiler family automatically. Clang links use
`-fuse-ld=lld` and support LLVM LTO, including ThinLTO. Changed libraries and
unsupported link options stay local. See distcc(1) for toolchain requirements.

For GCC and Clang compilation, `-march=native`, `-mtune=native` and
`-mcpu=native` are resolved by the client compiler before distribution.
The resolved CPU, enabled and disabled features, and GCC cache parameters
are used for both preprocessing and remote compilation. Explicit overrides
retain the compiler's normal precedence. Unrecognized expansions stay local.
Local-only invocations skip this probe. The broker reuses native expansions
for matching compiler, flags, environment, CPU affinity and configuration;
concurrent misses share one client-side probe. Wrappers, custom toolchain
configuration and response files use uncached probes. See distcc(1) for the
cache scope and invalidation rules.

The distcc "pump" functionality, added in distcc 3.0, improves on
distcc by distributing not only compilation but also preprocessing to
distcc servers. This requires that the server and client have the same
system headers (the client takes responsibility for transmitting
application-specific headers).  Given that, distcc in pump mode yields
the same results that distcc would in non-pump mode, but faster, since
the preprocessor no longer runs locally. For more details on the pump
functionality, see README.pump.

distcc is not itself a compiler, but rather a front-end to the GNU
C/C++ compiler (gcc), or another compiler of your choice. All the
regular gcc options and features work as normal.

distcc is designed to be used with GNU make's parallel-build feature
(-j). Shipping files across the network takes time, but few cycles on
the client machine. Any files that can be built remotely are
essentially "for free" in terms of client CPU.  This is even more true
in "pump" mode, where the client does not even have to take time to
preprocess the source files.  distcc has been successfully used in
environments with hundreds of distcc servers, supporting dozens of
simultaneous compiles.

distcc is now reasonably stable and can successfully compile the Linux
kernel, rsync, KDE, GNOME (via GARNOME), Samba and Ethereal.  distcc
is nearly linearly scalable for small numbers of machines: for a
typical case, three machines are 2.6 times faster than one.

